A `page.jsonl` page routes its children through the same `Page.child(name)` in `Page.class.js`, but a `page.jsonl` file line short-circuits the ordinary filesystem probe.

Here's the chain of reasoning:

1. **Declaring the link.** `Log.js`'s `file(name)` method (called by `set()` for every `{"file": "…"}` line) tests the name against `CHILD` — the regex `/^([^/]+)\/page\.(jsonl|js)$/`. A line like `{"file": "kid/page.jsonl"}` matches, so it calls `link_child(name)`, which in turn calls `child_file(entry)`. That records `this.child_kinds.set("kid", "jsonl")` (a Map on the page) and adds `"kid"` to `this.children` as an unresolved entry (`children.set(dir, null)`) if it isn't already there.

2. **Resolving the link at navigation time.** When the Router walks a url and calls `page.child("kid")` (`Page.class.js`), the ordinary order is: memory (`known = this.children.get(name)`), then `route()`, then a `page.js` probe (`Page.load`), then a `.md` fallback. But before the `route()`/probe steps, `child()` has this specific check:

```js
if (this.child_kinds?.get(name) === "jsonl") {
    const page = await Page.jsonl(this.url + name + "/");
    return page && this.add(name, page).load_all_children(levels);
}
```

So a child that was declared via a `kid/page.jsonl` file line is read straight through `Page.jsonl(url)` (in `Log.js`) — **no import probe, no guessing**. This is the "no probe" case doc/declaring.md and the jsonl doc call out explicitly: normal folders are discovered by trying `Page.load()` (a dynamic `import()`) and falling through to `.md`; a `page.jsonl`-linked child skips straight to the log reader because the page already told you what kind of file answers that name.

3. **What `Page.jsonl()` does.** It's a static on `PageLog` (`Log.js`): builds a `Reader` over `<url>page.jsonl`, awaits `reader.open()` (which streams live on localhost via `ext/JSONL/live.js`, or does a one-shot fetch off localhost), reads line 1 as the constructor args (optionally naming `"class"` to build a `Page` subclass instead of a plain `Page`/`Card`), sets `page.content ??= page.log_view`, and attaches the reader so later lines stream in via `set()`.

One more wrinkle worth naming: a `page.js` parent can declare the same link by hand in its `children:` string — `children: "generator jsonl/page.jsonl"` — and `declare()` routes that string through `this.child_file(child)` too (`Page.class.js`, the `declare()` method calls `child_file` on each string entry), which populates `child_kinds` the same way the jsonl log's own `file()` line does. So both a `page.js` parent and a `page.jsonl` parent end up feeding the identical `child_kinds` Map that `child()` checks.

A page.jsonl page routes children through the same `Page.child(name)` method every page uses (`Page.class.js`), but the log format pre-declares which names are children so `child()` never has to probe the filesystem for them.

Concretely:

1. **Linking happens on the way in, in `Log.js`.** Every `{"file": "..."}` line calls `file(name)`. `file()` tests the name against `CHILD = /^([^/]+)\/page\.(jsonl|js)$/`. A match (e.g. `"kid/page.jsonl"` or `"kid/page.js"`) calls `link_child(name)`, which calls `child_file(name)` to split it into `dir` ("kid") and `kind` ("jsonl" or "js"), records `this.child_kinds.set(dir, kind)`, and — if not already there — sets `this.children.set(dir, null)` (`Log.js`, `file()`/`child_file()`/`link_child()`). So by the time anyone routes, the page's `children` Map already has an entry for `kid`, and `child_kinds` remembers whether it's a `.jsonl` or `.js` child.

2. **Routing itself is `Page.child(name)` in `Page.class.js`.** The relevant branch:
```js
async child(name, levels){
    ...
    const known = this.children.get(name);
    if (known) return known.assign({ app: this.app }).load_all_children(levels);

    // A child the listing named as `kid/page.jsonl` is read from that file — no probe.
    if (this.child_kinds?.get(name) === "jsonl") {
        const page = await Page.jsonl(this.url + name + "/");
        return page && this.add(name, page).load_all_children(levels);
    }
    ...
}
```
So there are two cases:
   - **`kid/page.js`**: `child_kinds.get("kid")` is `"js"`, which doesn't match the jsonl branch, so `child()` falls through to the ordinary `Page.load(this.url + name + "/", 0)` probe — a dynamic `import(url + "page.js")`. A `page.js` child wins over a same-named `page.jsonl` beside it (stated in `doc/jsonl.md`'s "Three steps" section, step 2).
   - **`kid/page.jsonl`**: `child_kinds.get("kid")` is `"jsonl"`, so `child()` calls `Page.jsonl(this.url + name + "/")` directly — no import probe, no 404 risk. `Page.jsonl` is `PageLog.jsonl` (`Log.js`), which fetches `page.jsonl` via `PageLog.Reader`, builds line 1 as `new Class(line)` (Class is `this` unless line 1 names `"class"`), attaches the reader, and returns the page. Back in `child()`, that returned page is adopted with `this.add(name, page)`.

3. **`unlink_child(key)`** is the inverse — a `{"file": "kid/page.jsonl", "gone": true}` line calls `file()` then `gone(true)`, which deletes the `listed` entry and calls `unlink_child`, which deletes the `child_kinds` entry and removes it from `this.children` (`Log.js`).

So the one line of reasoning: **`link_child`/`child_kinds` (set by `file()` lines in `Log.js`) turns "route by probing the filesystem" into "route by reading a recorded kind," and `Page.child()`'s `child_kinds?.get(name) === "jsonl"` check (`Page.class.js`) is the actual routing fork that calls `Page.jsonl()` instead of `Page.load()`.**

A page.jsonl doesn't route its own children with special logic — it uses the same `child_kinds` map every Page has, just populated a different way.

Each `{"file": "kid/page.jsonl"}` line runs `set()` → `file()` (Log.js:50-53), which sees the name matches `CHILD` (`/^([^/]+)\/page\.(jsonl|js)$/`, Log.js:19) and calls `link_child()` (Log.js:74-77). `link_child` calls `child_file(name)` (Log.js:66-72), which records `this.child_kinds.set("kid", "jsonl")` and returns `"kid"`, then sets `this.children.set("kid", null)` — a declared-but-unresolved child, same shape a page.js's `declare()` produces for a plain string entry (Page.class.js:114).

Routing itself happens later, when something actually asks for that name via `Page.child(name, levels)` (Page.class.js:198-228). The relevant branch is line 206-208:

```js
if (this.child_kinds?.get(name) === "jsonl") {
    const page = await Page.jsonl(this.url + name + "/");
    return page && this.add(name, page).load_all_children(levels);
}
```

Because `child_kinds.get("kid")` is `"jsonl"`, `child()` skips the normal `route()`/`Page.load()`/probe chain entirely and calls the static loader `Page.jsonl(url)` (Log.js:206-220), which reads `kid/page.jsonl` via `PageLog.Reader`, builds the child page from its first line, and streams the rest as `set()` calls. The child is then adopted with `this.add(name, page)` (Page.class.js:140-153), same as any other child.

So: the jsonl format's only routing contribution is stamping `child_kinds` (a plain `Map<name, "jsonl"|"js">`) during `file()`/`link_child()`/`child_file()` in Log.js; the actual dispatch-on-name still lives in `Page.child()` in Page.class.js, which checks that map before falling through to `route()` and the filesystem probe. This is also why `child()`'s comment at Page.class.js:205 says "no probe" — the jsonl case is resolved from the map, never guessed.

A folder with a `page.js` exists as a page because `page.js` is a module that constructs itself at import — `export default new Page({...})` runs the moment the module loads, and importing it is exactly what `Page.load()` does:

```js
static async load(url, levels){
  try {
    const page = (await import(url + "page.js")).default ?? null;
    return page instanceof Page ? page.load_all_children(levels) : page;
  }
  ...
}
```

Nothing "discovers" the page in advance; the page is the side effect of a successful `import()`. There is no registry and no filesystem listing (`declaring.md`: "a browser still cannot list a directory") — resolving a url is a probe, not a lookup.

That probe is `child(name, levels)` in `Page.class.js`, and it runs in a fixed order:

1. **memory** — is `name` already in `this.children` as a built `Page`?
2. **`route(name)`** — only if `known === undefined`, so a declared child can never be shadowed by a catalogue route.
3. **the filesystem probe** — `await Page.load(this.url + name + "/", 0)`. This is the `import(url + "page.js")` call above.
4. **a `.md` file** — `Page.file(this.md_dir() + name + ".md")`, last, so a real `page.js` always wins over a doc with the same name.

If none of the four produces a page, `child()` returns `null`, and the Router's walk (`load_segments` in `Router.js`) stops there: `page = await page.child(name); if (!page) return null;` — that's the 404.

So a **declared child with no `page.js`** 404s because declaring a name (`children: "kid"`) only ever populates `this.children.set(name, null)` in `declare()` — a placeholder, not a page. When `child("kid")` is later called, step 1 finds `known = null` (falsy, so it doesn't short-circuit as "already resolved"), step 2's `route()` is skipped precisely because `known !== undefined` (declaring the name already answered "is this mine"), and step 3 tries to `import("kid/page.js")` — if that file genuinely isn't on disk, the dynamic import throws a module-not-found error, caught in `Page.load()`:

```js
catch (error){
  if (!Page.missing(error))
    console.error(...);   // only a REAL file that fails to load is logged
  return null;
}
```

`Page.missing(error)` pattern-matches the "failed to fetch/load dynamically imported module" family of messages, so a genuinely absent file returns `null` quietly (step 4 still gets a chance at a sibling `.md`, but if that's absent too, `child()` returns `null` and the url 404s in the Router).

The doc note in `declaring.md` names this precisely as the intended failure mode: *"A forgotten declaration should cost the menu entry, not the url"* — meaning the old fear (a typo'd child name silently 404s) is accepted as the trade for not needing a hand-maintained registry. The `Page.missing()` distinction is what keeps that 404 quiet instead of an alarming stack trace: a page that doesn't exist is different from a page whose `page.js` exists but has a syntax error or throws — the latter is *not* swallowed, and gets a `console.error` naming the real bug.
